Tracy: make the Linux dlclose() override optional (TRACY_NO_DLCLOSE_OVERRIDE)

On Linux, TracyClient.cpp defines a global dlclose() that does nothing, so that no shared object
is unloaded while the profiler may still resolve its symbols. That holds for the whole process:
in every engine build that links Tracy (Dev, and Release with DE_ENABLE_PROFILE) de_dylib_close()
stopped unloading libraries. That breaks the game library hot reload (DENG-93) and the
dylib.open_symbol_close test (a library opened again kept its globals; Linux CI, DENG-94).

The engine captures no callstacks and runs no sampling by default, so it does not need the
override. third_party/tracy/CMakeLists.txt defines TRACY_NO_DLCLOSE_OVERRIDE, and this patch
makes the override honour it; without the macro the file behaves as upstream. Engine-local patch
(DENG-94), applied by tools/vendor/vendor.py. Drop it when a pinned release has such a switch.

diff --git a/public/client/TracyOverride.cpp b/public/client/TracyOverride.cpp
--- a/public/client/TracyOverride.cpp
+++ b/public/client/TracyOverride.cpp
@@ -1,5 +1,5 @@
 #ifdef TRACY_ENABLE
-#  ifdef __linux__
+#  if defined( __linux__ ) && !defined( TRACY_NO_DLCLOSE_OVERRIDE )
 #    include "TracyDebug.hpp"
 #    ifdef TRACY_VERBOSE
 #      include <dlfcn.h>
